{
  "slug": "group-the-people-given-the-group-size-they-belong-to",
  "hints": [
    "People with the same group size can be handled together. What data structure can collect person IDs by that size?",
    "Whenever a bucket reaches its required size, that bucket is already a complete valid group.",
    "Each person should be added and emitted exactly once, so a single left-to-right pass is enough."
  ],
  "title": "Group the People Given the Group Size They Belong To Problem",
  "content": {
    "notes": [
      "Use person IDs from 0 to n - 1. The value groupSizes[i] determines the required size of the group containing person i.",
      "A bucket for a group size can be emitted as soon as it reaches that size. Processing people from left to right preserves a deterministic valid ordering.",
      "The input guarantees that every group size occurs a number of times that can be partitioned into complete groups of that size."
    ],
    "constraints": [
      "groupSizes.length == n",
      "1 <= n <= 500",
      "1 <= groupSizes[i] <= n"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Single line: JSON array of all arguments, e.g. [arg1, arg2, ...]",
    "outputFormat": "Single line: JSON-serialised return value",
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "[[3,3,3,3,3,1,3]]",
        "output": "[[5],[0,1,2],[3,4,6]]",
        "explanation": "Person 5 forms the size-1 group. The remaining people are completed into two size-3 groups, each containing only people whose required size is 3."
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_2",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "[[2,1,3,3,3,2]]",
        "output": "[[1],[0,5],[2,3,4]]",
        "explanation": "Person 1 needs a group of size 1, people 0 and 5 need a group of size 2, and people 2, 3, and 4 form the required size-3 group."
      }
    ],
    "problemStatement": "There are `n` people that are split into some unknown number of groups. Each person is labeled with a **unique ID** from `0` to `n - 1`.\n\nYou receive an integer array `groupSizes`, where `groupSizes[i]` is the size of the group that person `i` is in. As an example, if `groupSizes[1] = 3`, then person `1` must be in a group of size `3`.\n\nOutput *a list of groups such that each person `i` is in a group of size `groupSizes[i]`*.\n\nEach person should appear in **exactly one group**, and every person must be in a group. If there are multiple answers, **output any of them**. It is **guaranteed** that there will be **no fewer than one** valid solution for the given input.\n\n**Example 1:**\n\n```\nInput: groupSizes = [3,3,3,3,3,1,3]\nOutput: [[5],[0,1,2],[3,4,6]]\nExplanation: \nThe first group is [5]. The size is 1, and groupSizes[5] = 1.\nThe second group is [0,1,2]. The size is 3, and groupSizes[0] = groupSizes[1] = groupSizes[2] = 3.\nThe third group is [3,4,6]. The size is 3, and groupSizes[3] = groupSizes[4] = groupSizes[6] = 3.\nOther possible solutions are [[2,1,6],[5],[0,4,3]] and [[5],[0,6,2],[4,3,1]].\n```\n\n**Example 2:**\n\n```\nInput: groupSizes = [2,1,3,3,3,2]\nOutput: [[1],[0,5],[2,3,4]]\n```\n\n**Constraints:**\n\n* `groupSizes.length == n`\n* `1 <= n <= 500`\n* `1 <= groupSizes[i] <= n`"
  },
